I'm skeptical that this is a useful way of looking at the question.
Any individual spellbook's chances will look flat when compared to the
rest of them, and if a 10 point difference can be termed negligible,
why not the 40 some-odd point spread separating the most distant
probabilities for spellbooks, since neither of those numbers is really
significant (two orders of magnitude down) compared to the totality?

The useful way looking at this I *do* see at this is to consider how
the probability of any individual spellbook being generated maps into
the space of games that contain a given spellbook -- to ask the
question "At what number of generated spellbooks could I reasonably
expect I *will* see healing? What's that number for charm monster? For
create familiar?" At least informally you can make the statement that
you'd expect to see an occurance of healing at around 25 spellbooks,
charm monster at around 50 spellbooks, but you would not have the same
level of expectation for create familiar until around 100 spellbooks.
Obviously there are vagaries of random number generation that will
mean you will see games that differ from that expectation, perhaps
even some radically different, but most games probably stick closer to
those expectations.

And this is the place where I think it's easy to gauge significance.
Most games will have 25 spellbooks if you survive to the valley of the
dead, and it's probably not infrequent to see 25 spellbooks by half
that depth and/or 50 by some point in Gehennom. Few games will give
you 100 spellbooks by the Valley. There are probably many games (maybe
most) where there aren't 100 spellbooks created.

This is what I mean when I say that it appears to me that create
familiar is significantly less likely to appear than charm monster.

> Given that the most common/effective method of getting spellbooks is to
> polymorph a line of blessed ones (You should know this.  :)

It's true -- anyone who watches me play for not very long at all will
soon discover I'm a completely shameless polypiler. :) And past
certain points in the game, there's obviously several other means
available by which any player can get any particular spell they're
interested in.

But that's not really connected to the whole point of bringing this
up, which is that I think you'll see more people use create familiar
in interesting ways if it shows up more frequently earlier in the
game. Not just because I feel like it's less interesting to *me* in
the late game, though that's true and might be for others. I also
think there's a certain inertia at work whereby a number of items/
features get used less frequently in the late side of game because
players don't get trained to use them earlier in the game.

Perhaps few other players feel that way about the spell and I'm wrong
about that particular mechanic, but it's one explanation for why more
people may not have tinkered with it yet. There may be others. The
only thing I can say for certain is that's why I didn't bother with
the spell when I had the chance, and that I'll probably be interested
if the game comes up where I see it earlier.



